WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.099 --> 00:00:04.140
These days, Hollywood seems to operate under
the premise that you can never have too much

00:00:04.140 --> 00:00:05.240
of a good thing.

00:00:05.240 --> 00:00:09.830
If people like a film, well, that means you
can expect a dozen sequels to be announced.

00:00:09.830 --> 00:00:12.230
But sometimes things don't go as expected.

00:00:12.230 --> 00:00:17.450
Here's a look at some planned trilogies that,
sadly, will never be completed.

00:00:17.450 --> 00:00:19.160
Hellboy

00:00:19.160 --> 00:00:23.750
If there's an unmade sequel out there that
fans truly want to see, it's Hellboy III.

00:00:23.750 --> 00:00:29.030
Guillermo del Toro's Hellboy II: The Golden
Army was released in July 2008, starring Ron

00:00:29.030 --> 00:00:30.870
Perlman as the not-quite-horned-hero.

00:00:30.870 --> 00:00:37.019
It received overwhelmingly positive reviews
from critics and grossed over $160 million

00:00:37.019 --> 00:00:40.899
worldwide on an estimated $85 million production
budget.

00:00:40.899 --> 00:00:45.550
But even though the movie was a modest success
for del Toro, it wasn't a monster hit.

00:00:45.550 --> 00:00:49.010
So Universal Pictures never commissioned a
third installment.

00:00:49.010 --> 00:00:53.780
Since then, both del Toro and Perlman have
publicly lobbied to make a third Hellboy,

00:00:53.780 --> 00:00:55.389
but nothing has come of it.

00:00:55.389 --> 00:00:56.940
Still, hope springs eternal.

00:00:56.940 --> 00:01:02.230
In January of 2017, del Toro asked his Twitter
followers to weigh in on the possibility of

00:01:02.230 --> 00:01:04.089
a third Hellboy movie.

00:01:04.089 --> 00:01:10.250
After getting more than 132,000 responses,
del Toro and Perlman approached Hellboy creator

00:01:10.250 --> 00:01:13.340
Mike Mignola to discuss the possibility of
a third film.

00:01:13.340 --> 00:01:18.299
But there have been plenty of hopeful meetings
before, and after nearly a decade of waiting,

00:01:18.299 --> 00:01:22.030
it's starting to look like this one will never
actually happen.

00:01:22.030 --> 00:01:24.049
Amazing Spider-Man

00:01:24.049 --> 00:01:29.940
Despite critics and audiences largely detesting
Sam Raimi's Spider-Man 3, Tobey Maguire at

00:01:29.940 --> 00:01:32.770
least got the chance to conclude his Spider-Man
trilogy.

00:01:32.770 --> 00:01:38.070
That's something Andrew Garfield will never
get to do, because in 2015 Sony Pictures suspended

00:01:38.070 --> 00:01:42.909
plans to expand the Amazing Spider-Man franchise
and instead opted to share the character with

00:01:42.909 --> 00:01:43.909
Marvel Studios.

00:01:43.909 --> 00:01:48.869
As a result, we now have Tom Holland playing
a new Spider-Man in the Marvel Cinematic Universe

00:01:48.869 --> 00:01:53.340
and a rebooted Spider-Man film series that
officially kicks off with Spider-Man: Homecoming

00:01:53.340 --> 00:01:54.750
in 2017.

00:01:54.750 --> 00:01:57.120
But what were Sony's original plans?

00:01:57.120 --> 00:02:02.250
Prior to Marc Webb's The Amazing Spider-Man
2 in 2014, Sony planned to launch an extended

00:02:02.250 --> 00:02:05.810
Spider-Man franchise to compete with the Marvel
Cinematic Universe.

00:02:05.810 --> 00:02:10.510
It was going to include two more Amazing Spider-Man
films, as well as spin-offs for Venom and

00:02:10.510 --> 00:02:12.080
the Sinister Six.

00:02:12.080 --> 00:02:17.920
Sony also hired Westworld co-creator Lisa
Joy Nolan to write a script for a female superhero

00:02:17.920 --> 00:02:21.640
movie set in their Spider-Man universe, but
nothing has since come of it.

00:02:21.640 --> 00:02:25.830
Although the studio canceled plans to pursue
more Amazing Spider-Man films, they haven't

00:02:25.830 --> 00:02:28.030
shuttered all the Spider-Man spinoff films.

00:02:28.030 --> 00:02:32.450
In March 2016, Sony confirmed they were moving
forward with the Venom film.

00:02:32.450 --> 00:02:36.280
However, there don't appear to be plans to
have the movie connect to either the Amazing

00:02:36.280 --> 00:02:39.070
Spider-Man films or the Marvel Cinematic Universe.

00:02:39.070 --> 00:02:42.440
"So, to become an Avenger, are there like,
trials, or…"

00:02:42.440 --> 00:02:45.120
"Just don't do anything I would do.

00:02:45.120 --> 00:02:46.720
And definitely don't do anything I wouldn't
do.

00:02:46.720 --> 00:02:50.340
There's a little gray area in there and that's
where you operate."

00:02:50.340 --> 00:02:52.480
National Treasure

00:02:52.480 --> 00:02:59.271
Jon Turteltaub's lowbrow take on Indiana Jones
was a surprise hit in 2004, and the 2007 sequel

00:02:59.271 --> 00:03:04.950
National Treasure: Book of Secrets performed
even better, bringing in almost $460 million

00:03:04.950 --> 00:03:06.100
worldwide.

00:03:06.100 --> 00:03:10.230
The success prompted Disney to commission
a sequel the following year, and with the

00:03:10.230 --> 00:03:14.960
studio acquiring domain names for National
Treasure 3 and 4, rumors began to spread that

00:03:14.960 --> 00:03:17.560
the Mouse House was eyeing back-to-back sequels.

00:03:17.560 --> 00:03:21.650
Although Turtletaub was interested in making
another National Treasure film, he said they

00:03:21.650 --> 00:03:26.030
were going to take their time and get the
story right before moving forward, explaining

00:03:26.030 --> 00:03:30.200
in an interview, "[U]ntil we have a great
story, a great adventure and a great piece

00:03:30.200 --> 00:03:33.370
of history to explore, there's no point in
making the movie."

00:03:33.370 --> 00:03:36.670
A decade later, it still hasn't happened.

00:03:36.670 --> 00:03:37.670
TRON

00:03:37.670 --> 00:03:43.370
It took almost 30 years, but in 2010 Disney
finally released a sequel to their 1982 cult

00:03:43.370 --> 00:03:45.010
classic TRON.

00:03:45.010 --> 00:03:49.650
Starring Garrett Hedlund, Olivia Wilde, and
Jeff Bridges, TRON: Legacy earned average

00:03:49.650 --> 00:03:52.710
critic reviews and modest box office success.

00:03:52.710 --> 00:03:57.130
It wasn't a failure, but it wasn't a big enough
hit for Disney to move forward with a third

00:03:57.130 --> 00:03:58.130
film.

00:03:58.130 --> 00:04:03.240
But the thing is, TRON: Legacy writers Edward
Kitsis and Adam Horowitz had already begun

00:04:03.240 --> 00:04:07.100
working on a script for a third film before
Legacy even released.

00:04:07.100 --> 00:04:11.650
Not long after Legacy hit, Ain't It Cool News
reported that Disney was gearing up to announce

00:04:11.650 --> 00:04:14.200
TRON 3 — but the announcement never arrived.

00:04:14.200 --> 00:04:18.470
Instead, after five years of dragging their
feet, Disney finally announced the series

00:04:18.470 --> 00:04:19.470
was dead.

00:04:19.470 --> 00:04:20.660
But hey, who knows?

00:04:20.660 --> 00:04:23.850
Maybe they'll bring it back again in thirty
more years.

00:04:23.850 --> 00:04:25.360
Kick-Ass

00:04:25.360 --> 00:04:30.090
Director Matthew Vaughn and star Aaron Taylor-Johnson
brought the comic book series Kick-Ass to

00:04:30.090 --> 00:04:32.420
life on the big screen in 2008.

00:04:32.420 --> 00:04:36.970
Praised for its adult themes, Kick-Ass received
positive reviews from critics and managed

00:04:36.970 --> 00:04:41.780
to become a modest box office success, prompting
Universal to move forward with a sequel.

00:04:41.780 --> 00:04:47.740
Kick-Ass 2, meanwhile, was released in August
2013 to negative reviews and declining box

00:04:47.740 --> 00:04:48.740
office sales.

00:04:48.740 --> 00:04:53.000
Shortly before the release of Kick-Ass 2,
the comic book's writer confirmed that plans

00:04:53.000 --> 00:04:57.150
for a third movie would largely depend upon
how much money the second movie earned.

00:04:57.150 --> 00:05:02.661
Later that month, reports of plans for a third
installment surfaced, saying Kick-Ass 3 would

00:05:02.661 --> 00:05:05.630
conclude the Kick-Ass trilogy with a major
death.

00:05:05.630 --> 00:05:09.970
After the film's disappointing results, though,
those plans were put on hold along with a

00:05:09.970 --> 00:05:11.350
rumored Hit Girl prequel.

00:05:11.350 --> 00:05:13.490
That... does not kick ass.

00:05:13.490 --> 00:05:15.840
28 Months Later

00:05:15.840 --> 00:05:22.150
Five years after Danny Boyle's 28 Days Later
hit theaters, the sequel 28 Weeks Later arrived,

00:05:22.150 --> 00:05:27.410
depicting a NATO takeover of the U.K., where
they supposedly quelled a zombie infestation.

00:05:27.410 --> 00:05:31.800
Unfortunately, one person infected with the
virus survived and managed to spread it to

00:05:31.800 --> 00:05:33.120
the remaining survivors.

00:05:33.120 --> 00:05:37.890
At the end of the movie, a handful of survivors
escape England and make it to France.

00:05:37.890 --> 00:05:42.000
However, one of them was still a carrier for
the infection, allowing the infection to spread

00:05:42.000 --> 00:05:45.040
across the channel, ending the film on a cliffhanger.

00:05:45.040 --> 00:05:49.910
Shortly after 28 Weeks Later released, Boyle
indicated that plans for a third installment

00:05:49.910 --> 00:05:51.370
were in the works.

00:05:51.370 --> 00:05:56.540
Unfortunately, screenwriter Alex Garland believes
the rumored 28 Months Later movie won't happen

00:05:56.540 --> 00:06:01.430
due to copyright issues: "When we made 28
Days Later, the rights were frozen between

00:06:01.430 --> 00:06:04.410
a group of people who are no longer talking
to each other."

00:06:04.410 --> 00:06:08.930
Still, Garland and Boyle are still talking
about doing a third film, if those legal issues

00:06:08.930 --> 00:06:10.250
can ever get resolved.

00:06:10.250 --> 00:06:11.570
We'll see.

00:06:11.570 --> 00:06:13.110
Ghostbusters

00:06:13.110 --> 00:06:17.080
The first Ghostbusters movie ranks as one
of the most beloved films in history.

00:06:17.080 --> 00:06:22.120
In fact, the U.S. Library of Congress selected
the original Ghostbusters for preservation

00:06:22.120 --> 00:06:28.480
in the National Film Registry in 2015 for
being "culturally, historically, or aesthetically

00:06:28.480 --> 00:06:30.210
significant."

00:06:30.210 --> 00:06:34.660
It became a worldwide phenomenon following
its 1984 release, which prompted Columbia

00:06:34.660 --> 00:06:36.570
Pictures to commission a sequel.

00:06:36.570 --> 00:06:41.020
And while Ghostbusters II wasn't as well received
by critics as the first movie, it was still

00:06:41.020 --> 00:06:48.500
a hit, earning over $215 million worldwide
on an estimated $37 million production budget.

00:06:48.500 --> 00:06:51.400
"We came, we saw, we kicked its ass!"

00:06:51.400 --> 00:06:56.110
Despite the success, however, director Ivan
Reitman didn't have any immediate plans to

00:06:56.110 --> 00:06:57.470
make a third installment.

00:06:57.470 --> 00:07:03.850
Flash forward 10 years, and series star and
writer Dan Aykroyd finished a 122-page draft

00:07:03.850 --> 00:07:05.840
for Ghostbusters 3: Hellbent.

00:07:05.840 --> 00:07:10.139
It appeared that they were finally making
progress on a third film, but Bill Murray

00:07:10.139 --> 00:07:13.670
was reportedly uninterested in making another
Ghostbusters.

00:07:13.670 --> 00:07:18.270
It was then rumored that Ben Stiller could
replace Murray in the sequel, alongside Aykroyd

00:07:18.270 --> 00:07:19.280
and Harold Ramis.

00:07:19.280 --> 00:07:20.280
"Venkman!

00:07:20.280 --> 00:07:21.780
What happened, are you okay?!"

00:07:21.780 --> 00:07:23.040
"He slimed me."

00:07:23.040 --> 00:07:24.419
Nothing came of the idea, though.

00:07:24.419 --> 00:07:29.550
And after the death of Egon-actor Harold Ramis
in 2014, Reitman officially backed out of

00:07:29.550 --> 00:07:33.660
directing Ghostbusters 3, though he agreed
to stay on as a producer.

00:07:33.660 --> 00:07:37.970
After approaching a few directors, Sony decided
to move forward with a soft reboot of the

00:07:37.970 --> 00:07:39.410
franchise instead.

00:07:39.410 --> 00:07:43.190
Without Ramis, it looks as though the original
team has busted its last ghost.

00:07:43.190 --> 00:07:47.920
Regardless of the controversy surrounding
the 2016 reboot, at least there were seven

00:07:47.920 --> 00:07:52.919
seasons of The Real Ghostbusters, one season
of Extreme Ghostbusters, and 2009's Ghostbusters:

00:07:52.919 --> 00:07:58.990
The Video Game that tell more stories featuring
our favorite paranormal investigators and

00:07:58.990 --> 00:08:00.570
eliminators.

00:08:00.570 --> 00:08:02.280
Fantastic Four

00:08:02.280 --> 00:08:07.590
After achieving global success with the X-Men
series, 20th Century Fox hoped to build off

00:08:07.590 --> 00:08:11.639
those movies' hype by moving forward with
a Fantastic Four series.

00:08:11.639 --> 00:08:16.150
The first Fantastic Four movie released in
2005 to mixed reviews.

00:08:16.150 --> 00:08:21.020
However, fan anticipation led the Fantastic
Four to become a box office success, earning

00:08:21.020 --> 00:08:23.419
over three times its production budget.

00:08:23.419 --> 00:08:28.670
That success inevitably led Fox to commission
a sequel, titled Fantastic Four: Rise of the

00:08:28.670 --> 00:08:30.110
Silver Surfer.

00:08:30.110 --> 00:08:35.329
Director Tim Story and the rest of the cast
and crew all returned for the 2007 follow-up.

00:08:35.329 --> 00:08:40.419
But it was meh at best, earning mixed reviews
and mediocre box office.

00:08:40.419 --> 00:08:45.389
Story wanted to move forward with a third
film, but Fox decided against it, opting instead

00:08:45.389 --> 00:08:50.459
to put the franchise on hold before eventually
rebooting it in 2015 with Josh Trank.

00:08:50.459 --> 00:08:54.190
Of course, that move backfired, well, fantastically.

00:08:54.190 --> 00:08:57.999
The new version became a critical punching
bag and a box office disaster.

00:08:57.999 --> 00:09:00.399
Should have stuck with the original after
all...

00:09:00.399 --> 00:09:03.450
"That's gross."

00:09:03.450 --> 00:09:04.740
Kill Bill

00:09:04.740 --> 00:09:09.430
Quentin Tarantino likes to make a single story
at a time, moving from one project to another

00:09:09.430 --> 00:09:11.360
without thinking about sequels.

00:09:11.360 --> 00:09:15.921
However, just a few short months prior to
Kill Bill releasing in theaters, Tarantino

00:09:15.921 --> 00:09:20.639
and Miramax concluded that his four-hour film
would have to be split into two releases.

00:09:20.639 --> 00:09:24.360
Thus Kill Bill: Volume 1 and Kill Bill: Volume
2 were born.

00:09:24.360 --> 00:09:28.050
Although they aren't his most commercially
successful films, the series has become a

00:09:28.050 --> 00:09:30.230
cult hit in the years since its release.

00:09:30.230 --> 00:09:34.800
The second film ends on an unexpected up note,
with Beatrix and her daughter heading off

00:09:34.800 --> 00:09:36.499
to live a life of peace.

00:09:36.499 --> 00:09:39.240
However, Tarantino believes there's always
room for more.

00:09:39.240 --> 00:09:44.930
In 2004, he indicated plans to make a Volume
3 with Uma Thurman reprising her role as the

00:09:44.930 --> 00:09:49.959
Bride, though he said he would probably wait
around 15 years before making another one.

00:09:49.959 --> 00:09:55.160
"You and I have unfinished business."

00:09:55.160 --> 00:09:58.629
"Baby… you ain't kidding."

00:09:58.629 --> 00:10:02.920
Over the next few years, Tarantino hinted
he still had plans to make a third Kill Bill,

00:10:02.920 --> 00:10:07.600
with the story potentially focusing on "the
revenge of two killers whose arms and eyes

00:10:07.600 --> 00:10:12.570
were hacked by Uma Thurman in the first stories,"
or maybe a revenge story featuring the daughter

00:10:12.570 --> 00:10:16.250
of Vernita Green turning the tables and hunting
down Thurman.

00:10:16.250 --> 00:10:18.430
But nothing ever came of either idea.

00:10:18.430 --> 00:10:23.170
In 2012, Tarantino finally conceded: "I don't
know if there's ever going to be a Kill Bill

00:10:23.170 --> 00:10:24.170
Vol. 3."

00:10:24.170 --> 00:10:27.570
And that, of course, led Tarantino's fans
to do this:

00:10:28.800 --> 00:10:30.940
(screaming)

00:10:31.600 --> 00:10:32.440
Thanks for watching!

00:10:32.449 --> 00:10:35.209
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:10:35.209 --> 00:10:37.730
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

